# home_assist — copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored; this file is not.
#
# The minimum to boot: HOMEASSIST_ADMIN_* and the MYSQL_* block.
# Everything else has a working default. See README.md § Setup for the full reference.
#
# NOTE: values here are only DEFAULTS. Once the app has run, the water_settings table wins,
# and those are edited on the Settings page without a redeploy.
#
# NEVER set a key twice in .env. dotenv keeps the FIRST occurrence and silently discards every
# later one, so an edit at the bottom of the file does nothing at all. Check with:
#   node src/home_assist/admin.js envcheck     (reports duplicates + anything missing)
#   node src/home_assist/admin.js envfix       (de-duplicates, keeping the LAST value, backs up first)

# ─────────────────────────── WEB SERVER ───────────────────────────
HOMEASSIST_PORT=
# Override where the built SPA is served from (default: src/home_assist/web/dist)
# HOMEASSIST_WEB_DIST=

# ─────────────────────────── AUTH (required) ──────────────────────
# Recovery admin — ALWAYS valid, never removable, so you can't lock yourself out of
# your own house dashboard. BOTH must be set for the account to count.
#
# A STORED user of the same name (added on the Admin page) is checked too, and would keep
# working after you change the password here. `admin.js auth <user> <password>` says which
# source accepted a login.
HOMEASSIST_ADMIN_USER=
HOMEASSIST_ADMIN_PASS=

# Optional second admin (both required)
# HOMEASSIST_TEST_USER=
# HOMEASSIST_TEST_PASS=

# Cookie-signing key. If unset, one is generated into auth.json on first run.
# Set it explicitly if you want to be able to rotate it. Changing it logs everyone out.
# HOMEASSIST_SESSION_SECRET=

# Where auth.json / panel_access.json / captures live. Default is per-platform —
# `node src/home_assist/admin.js where` prints the resolved paths.
# HOMEASSIST_DATA_DIR=
# Point at individual files instead (mainly for tests):
# HOMEASSIST_USERS_FILE=
# HOMEASSIST_PANEL_ACCESS_FILE=

# ─────────────────────────── MYSQL (required) ─────────────────────
# `npm run db_init` creates the database and tables.
MYSQL_HOST=
MYSQL_PORT=
MYSQL_USER=
MYSQL_PASSWORD=
MYSQL_DATABASE=
MYSQL_CONNECTION_LIMIT=

# ─────────────────────────── EMAIL ALERTS ─────────────────────────
# The primary alert channel. Gmail SMTP + an APP password generated under 2-factor auth —
# your normal account password will NOT work.
#   Google Account -> Security -> 2-Step Verification -> App passwords -> Mail
#
# Leave EMAIL_PASSWORD blank and everything still runs: leaks are recorded, nobody is told.
# `node collector_water.js --check` reports the state.
#
# EMAIL_RECIPIENT accepts several addresses, comma-separated. A per-meter address on the
# Meters page OVERRIDES this list for that meter — it replaces it, it does not add to it.

# ── WHICH ADDRESS THE ALERTS COME FROM ────────────────────────────
# Two options. BOTH send through Gmail; only the visible From address differs.
#
# ⚠ Exactly ONE EMAIL_SENDER and ONE EMAIL_USER may be uncommented. dotenv keeps the FIRST
#   occurrence of a key and silently ignores every later one, so a leftover uncommented line
#   wins and the edit below it does nothing at all.
#   Check with: node src/home_assist/admin.js envcheck

# ── Option A — send as your own Gmail ─────────────────────────────
# Fine while alerts only go to YOU. Mail to anyone else is likely to be filtered: nothing
# vouches for a personal Gmail account sending automated notices about someone else's water,
# and it is dropped SILENTLY — the alert ledger would read "delivered" while nobody was told.
# EMAIL_SENDER=you@gmail.com
# EMAIL_USER=

# ── Option B — send as your own domain ────────────────────────────
# Use this once any meter's alert address belongs to someone else. Depends on all three:
#   1. Gmail -> Settings -> Accounts -> "Send mail as" <alerts@yourdomain>, configured to send
#      through smtp.gmail.com. Gmail will pre-fill your domain's MX servers here — those only
#      RECEIVE mail and will not work. Overwrite them.
#   2. An inbound route for that address (Cloudflare Email Routing is free) forwarding it to
#      your Gmail, so Gmail's confirmation link, replies and bounces land somewhere.
#   3. SPF on the domain naming Google as an allowed sender. The TXT value reads:
#         "v=spf1  include:_spf.mx.cloudflare.net  include:_spf.google.com  ~all"
#      ONE SPF record only — two make both invalid. Merge, never add a second.
#      (Written with the quotes and spaced out on purpose: envcheck parses commented lines
#       leniently to spot documented-but-unset keys, and a bare v=spf1... reads to it as a
#       key called "v".)
# EMAIL_SENDER=alerts@yourdomain.org
# EMAIL_USER=you@gmail.com

# The SMTP LOGIN, which is only the same as the From address on Gmail. Blank = use
# EMAIL_SENDER, so an existing Gmail-only setup needs no change. Every transactional provider
# separates the two: Resend's username is the literal word "resend", Mailgun's is
# postmaster@<domain>, SES issues generated credentials.
EMAIL_USER=

# The DISPLAY NAME beside the From address, e.g. "Water Monitor <alerts@yourdomain.org>".
# Blank falls back to the machine's hostname, which answers "which box sent this?" without opening
# the mail — useful while YOU are the only recipient, and the wrong answer once a neighbour is one.
# A hostname in the From line reads as technical noise at best, and as something to report as spam
# at worst. Set it before any alert address belongs to someone else.
EMAIL_FROM_NAME=

EMAIL_SENDER=
EMAIL_PASSWORD=
EMAIL_RECIPIENT=

# Only if you are NOT using Gmail at all. Defaults: smtp.gmail.com, port 587, secure false.
# Set secure true only for port 465.
#
# Example, a dedicated sending service instead of Gmail:
#   EMAIL_HOST=smtp.resend.com
#   EMAIL_PORT=587
#   EMAIL_USER=resend
#   EMAIL_PASSWORD=<the API key>
#   EMAIL_SENDER=alerts@yourdomain.org
# EMAIL_HOST=
# EMAIL_PORT=
# EMAIL_SECURE=

# ─────────────────────────── PUSH (optional) ──────────────────────
# ntfy is a free phone-push service, off by default — useful because a 3am leak alert should
# wake you. The topic name is the only secret, so make it random. Email stays the primary
# channel. Also flip alert_ntfy_enabled to 1 on the Settings page.
NTFY_SERVER=
NTFY_TOPIC=

# ─────────────────────────── WATER MODULE ─────────────────────────
# Badger Orion classic pit transmitter, FCC ID GIF2006B.
# The radio id is NOT the serial printed on the endpoint (~857xxxxx) — see
# src/home_assist/plans_and_notes/water/HARDWARE.md.
WATER_METER_ID=
WATER_GALLONS_PER_UNIT=

# Local time for every hour bucket and the overnight window. Deliberately NOT the process
# timezone, so moving between machines can't silently shift the window.
# Example: America/Denver
WATER_TZ=

# rtl_433 decoder. Protocol 223 = "Badger ORION water meter, 100kbps", fixed 916.45 MHz.
# The classic Orion does NOT frequency-hop; 282/290 are the newer hopping Endpoint models.
#
# Platform-suffixed, same convention as wrestling_stats' .env
# (GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS_LINUX / _MAC / _WINDOWS). ONE .env works on both machines —
# nothing to comment out when you move between the laptop and the Ubuntu box.
#
# Windows: the STATIC exe, by full path. The nightly folder also holds rtl_433.exe, the DYNAMIC
#          build, which loads librtlsdr.dll from beside it — and that DLL looks patched (there is
#          an orig-librtlsdr.dll backup next to it), so the two are not interchangeable. The static
#          exe is the exact binary that decoded your meter in the 2026-07-31 hose test.
#          Do NOT keep it in OneDrive: Files On-Demand can dehydrate the .exe and the collector
#          then fails intermittently.
# Linux:   `make install` puts it on PATH at /usr/local/bin/rtl_433, so the bare name is enough.
WATER_RTL433_CMD_WINDOWS=
WATER_RTL433_CMD_LINUX=
# WATER_RTL433_CMD_MAC=

# Set the unsuffixed name to override every platform. An EMPTY value counts as unset, so a
# leftover `WATER_RTL433_CMD=` cannot silently defeat the platform lines above.
# WATER_RTL433_CMD=

# `node collector_water.js --check` verifies whichever one applies resolves, reports WHICH key
# it came from, and confirms the build includes protocol 223.

# -M level supplies Modulation, Frequency, RSSI, SNR and Noise — all five, from the one flag.
# Drop it and the Diagnostics signal columns and the SNR badge go blank.
#
# There is NO `-M freq`. It is not a valid value, and passing it makes rtl_433 suppress the
# WHOLE metadata set rather than ignore it — which presents as a dead antenna. Valid values are
# time | protocol | level | noise | stats | bits; `rtl_433 -M help` is the authority.
WATER_RTL433_ARGS=-f 916.45M -s 1600k -R 223 -F json -M level

# Manual gain: append `-g 28` (dB) to the line above. Worth trying when a strong neighbouring AMI
# mesh is desensitising the front end and a weak meter stops decoding — auto gain optimises for the
# loudest signal, which is not yours. `rtl_test` lists the tuner's supported steps.

# Two values (spaced off the '=' so envcheck does not read these lines as keys):
#   live    = spawn rtl_433 and read the real meter
#   replay  = synthetic meter or a captured file, through the same ingest path.
#          Lets you build and demo the UI with no dongle attached.
#          `--replay` on the command line does the same thing.
WATER_COLLECTOR_MODE=
# WATER_REPLAY_FILE=
